xnu 0204: the ring wraps without re-running the previous lap

Measured with the 0203 kernel: the 2000-submission bring-up stress refused
1373 of 2000 (`cp uc stress 2000 refused 1373 ... wptr 8186`), and the first
client submission found `at 0x1ffa rptr 0x19 wptr 0x0` and never moved. The
old wrap filled the tail with a `CP_NOP` but then published a write pointer
of zero and waited for the read pointer to come back to zero. With zero
published while the start still held the previous lap, the CP executed those
dwords again: its read pointer ran to 25, the wait for zero never matched,
the wrap returned -1, and every later submission failed (Mesa: `submit
failed: -5`).

Fill the tail, but publish the write pointer only with the submission that
is written at the start: the CP crosses the `CP_NOP` and then reads new data,
so it cannot run ahead into a lap it has already seen. Add the backpressure
the ring never had as well -- space to the read pointer before a submission
overwrites it -- because a client that submits faster than the CP consumes
could otherwise overrun the ring.

The per-submission log gets its own counter: `uc pre` and `uc big` shared
one, so after eight submissions neither printed and a wedged client stream
left no completion line at all.
